A custom view must tell the coder it has been replaced.

-[NSCustomView initWithCoder:] returns a DIFFERENT object from the one the unarchiver allocated,
and the unarchiver stores that raw allocation in its table BEFORE calling us, deliberately, so that
a cycle terminates. Anything that resolves this reference while the method is still running
therefore receives the bare NSCustomView. It never calls [super initWithCoder:], so that object has
nothing: frame zero, autoresizing mask zero, translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints NO, no
subviews.

The decode of NSSubviews is where the re-entrant reference is taken, so the swap has to come before
it. -[NSClassSwapper initWithCoder:] is the only other caller of replaceObject:withObject: and does
the same thing for the same reason. The unarchiver already expects a slot that no longer holds what
it allocated: the branch reading inSlot != instance && inSlot == initialised is written for exactly
this and drops the extra retain.

PROVEN BY POINTER, not inferred. iTerm2 Preferences decodes
iTermPreferencesInnerTabContainerView, the real view is built, and the object actually wired into
the view tree is the bare one:

  CIDER_CUSTOMVIEW name=iTermPreferencesInnerTabContainerView self=0x7128abd28120 new=0x7128abd28430
  CIDER_VIEW       NSCustomView(0x7128abd28120) 0x0 at 0,0 mask=0x0 translates=0 cons=0

The tree pointer and the placeholder self are the same address. Finding that is what the pointers
added to both traces here are for.

WHAT IT COST AND WHAT IT FIXES. iTerm2 sizes its Settings window to that pane, so a pane of zero
collapsed the whole window to the height of its own chrome: toolbar, tab row, and nothing under it.

  before   NSView 644x8    NSCustomView 0x0                        window 689x141
  after    NSView 644x151  iTermPreferencesInnerTabContainerView    window 689x284
                             569x143 at 38,15 mask=0xd translates=1
                             NSTextField, NSButton, NSButton, NSButton, NSButton

The General to Startup pane now draws its Window restoration policy label, its popup button and its
three checkboxes. Six other explanations for the zero sized pane were measured and refuted first;
they are listed in cocotron patch 0091 and in docs/iterm2-preferences-gap.md.

